{
 "id": "page-stand-sheet-science",
 "target": "page",
 "page": "stand-sheet.html",
 "goal": "Enter the stand sheet whole and hold what it computes. BLANK MEANS BLANK: aspect, slope, position, texture, moisture and every cover field stay out of the export until touched, and the method parameters that DO have defaults -- 5 cm minimum tallied DBH, 1.37 m breast height -- are in it from the start. The key narrows 34 species to 16 in the Sierra, 7 in bundles, 4 at five per bundle, 2 matching 'white'; a pack missing its required fields is rejected whole and a good one adds a species and a region. Four live stems -- three Douglas-fir at 30, 40 and 20 cm and a ponderosa at 25 -- on the default 11.28 m circle (400 m2, EF 25.0) give 100 stems/ha, 6.9 m2/ha live basal area, QMD 29.7 cm, SDI 132, and Douglas-fir leads on importance value 78.6 against the pine's 21.4; a 5.64 m radius is 100 m2 at EF 100.1, not 100.0. Aspect SW folds to 180 and reads hot, NW to 90 and warm, NE to 0 and cool (McCune & Keon). Coarse woody debris 12 8 31 45 9 cm on 30 m is 134.7 m3/ha by van Wagner and half that on 60 m. A 39.4312/-120.2381 fix with 30 m of GPS on a 11.28 m plot is 47 m of coordinate uncertainty, 5406 m with the datum unknown. Two interactions recorded, one of them seen; the field sheet carries every figure.",
 "policy": {
  "allow": [
   "SENSITIVE_READ",
   "DRAFT",
   "MUTATE",
   "DESTRUCTIVE"
  ],
  "needs": [
   "undo"
  ],
  "why": "the goal includes undoing the last tally: tUndo is 'Undo'"
 },
